/*
The statement as provided is incomplete/ambiguous, so below is the most likely intended
interpretation that matches the phrase "return the probability multiplied by N! modulo MOD":

- There are N lamps, exactly A are initially on.
- A random permutation p of {1..N} is chosen uniformly.
- If lamp i is on, you may press button i, which toggles lamp p[i].
- You play optimally to maximize the probability of eventually turning all lamps off.
- Output: (winning probability) * N! mod MOD.

Under this interpretation, the outcome depends only on the directed functional graph of p.
A component is a directed cycle with in-trees feeding into it.

Key fact:
You can always eliminate all tree nodes. The only obstruction is on each directed cycle:
starting from the current states on the cycle, allowed moves on cycle vertices correspond to
adding vectors e_{next(i)} while requiring current vertex i to be 1 before using it.
For a cycle, all lamps can be turned off iff the parity (xor) of initial states on that cycle is 0.
Thus the whole permutation is winnable iff every cycle contains an even number of initially-on lamps.

Initially lamps 1..A are on and A+1..N are off.
So for a permutation p, success iff every cycle contains an even count of elements from {1..A}.

Therefore:
answer = number of permutations of [N] such that every cycle contains an even number of elements
from the distinguished set of size A.

This count can be computed by exponential generating functions:
- A cycle with k distinguished and l ordinary elements contributes possible iff k is even.
- The EGF for allowed cycles is:
    C(x,y) = sum_{m>=1} 1/m * ( (x+y)^m + (y-x)^m ) / 2  - sum_{m>=1} y^m/m
           = -1/2 ln(1-(x+y)) -1/2 ln(1-(y-x)) + ln(1-y)
- Permutations are SETs of cycles, so EGF:
    P(x,y) = exp(C) = (1-y) / sqrt((1-y)^2 - x^2)

Then the required count is:
    N! * [x^A y^(N-A)] P(x,y)

Let B = N-A. Expand:
    P = (1-y) / sqrt((1-y)^2 - x^2)
      = 1 / sqrt(1 - x^2/(1-y)^2)
      = sum_{t>=0} binom(2t,t)/4^t * x^{2t} * (1-y)^{-2t}
So coefficient is zero for odd A. For A=2t:
    count = N! * [y^B] binom(2t,t)/4^t * (1-y)^{-2t}
          = N! * binom(2t,t)/4^t * binom(B+2t-1, 2t-1), for t>0
For A=0, count = N! (all permutations succeed).

Since the problem asks for probability * N! mod MOD, that is exactly this count modulo MOD.

This solution runs in O(N) preprocessing for factorials/inverses.
*/

public class Main {
    static final long MOD = 1_000_000_007L;

    static long modPow(long a, long e) {
        long r = 1 % MOD;
        a %= MOD;
        while (e > 0) {
            if ((e & 1) == 1) r = r * a % MOD;
            a = a * a % MOD;
            e >>= 1;
        }
        return r;
    }

    static long[] fact, invFact;

    static long C(int n, int k) {
        if (k < 0 || k > n) return 0;
        return fact[n] * invFact[k] % MOD * invFact[n - k] % MOD;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        FastScanner fs = new FastScanner(System.in);
        int N = fs.nextInt();
        int A = fs.nextInt();

        fact = new long[N + 1];
        invFact = new long[N + 1];
        fact[0] = 1;
        for (int i = 1; i <= N; i++) fact[i] = fact[i - 1] * i % MOD;
        invFact[N] = modPow(fact[N], MOD - 2);
        for (int i = N; i >= 1; i--) invFact[i - 1] = invFact[i] * i % MOD;

        long ans;
        if (A == 0) {
            ans = fact[N];
        } else if ((A & 1) == 1) {
            ans = 0;
        } else {
            int t = A / 2;
            int B = N - A;

            long central = C(2 * t, t);
            long inv4t = modPow(modPow(4, t), MOD - 2);
            long waysY = C(B + 2 * t - 1, 2 * t - 1);

            ans = fact[N];
            ans = ans * central % MOD;
            ans = ans * inv4t % MOD;
            ans = ans * waysY % MOD;
        }

        System.out.println(ans % MOD);
    }

    static class FastScanner {
        private final InputStream in;
        private final byte[] buffer = new byte[1 << 16];
        private int ptr = 0, len = 0;

        FastScanner(InputStream is) {
            in = is;
        }

        private int read() throws IOException {
            if (ptr >= len) {
                len = in.read(buffer);
                ptr = 0;
                if (len <= 0) return -1;
            }
            return buffer[ptr++];
        }

        int nextInt() throws IOException {
            int c;
            do c = read(); while (c <= ' ' && c != -1);
            int sign = 1;
            if (c == '-') {
                sign = -1;
                c = read();
            }
            int val = 0;
            while (c > ' ') {
                val = val * 10 + (c - '0');
                c = read();
            }
            return val * sign;
        }
    }
}
